 {"t": "Bearing Replacement Procedure", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Bearings fail by fatigue, contamination, and bad fits - and most replacement bearings fail early from installation damage, not from the original cause. This record covers the full procedure: confirming the bearing is the fault, selecting the exact replacement (designation, clearance, seals), correct removal without shaft damage, proper mounting (heat or press, never hammer blows through the rolling elements), and post-installation checks.\nThe procedure includes reading bearing designations, clearance selection (CN, C3), seal choices for the environment, shaft and housing fit checks, and the lubrication fill appropriate to speed.",
  "h": "Bearing replacement works when the mounting preserves the bearing's internal geometry. Force must pass through the ring being fitted - pressing on the outer ring to fit the inner ring drives the load through the balls and brinells the races. Induction heating the bearing for shaft fits expands it evenly without hot spots; the temperature limit protects the steel's temper and the seals. Clearance selection matters because operating temperature closes the internal clearance: a bearing that is correct cold can run tight hot. Post-installation, the checks are feel (smooth rotation), sound, and temperature trend - a new bearing that runs hot is telling the installer something is wrong.",
  "f": ["Confirm the bearing is the fault (vibration, temperature, sound) before replacing.",
        "Read the full designation: size, clearance, seals - substitutes need approval.",
        "Apply mounting force only through the ring being fitted; never through the rolling elements.",
        "Heat-mount with controlled induction heat; respect the temperature limit for seals and steel.",
        "Check shaft and housing fits and condition - a damaged seat kills the new bearing.",
        "Verify smooth rotation, normal sound, and temperature trend after installation."],
  "r": ["Exact replacement bearing: designation, clearance class, seal type.",
        "Bearing puller, induction heater or press; never strike through rolling elements.",
        "Micrometers for shaft and housing fit verification.",
        "Specified lubricant and fill quantity for the speed and size."],
  "s": [5, 6, 0], "c": [4, 5, 3],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Designation matches", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Mounting force through fitted ring only", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Heating within limit C", 110.0, 95.0, 15.0), ("Rotation smooth by hand", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Running temperature normal", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Sensor Recalibration After Repair", "g": "Adjustment", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Any repair that disturbs a sensor - or the mechanics around it - invalidates its calibration. This record defines the recalibration sequence after repair: identify every affected sensor, restore its mechanical installation (position, gap, alignment), apply the calibration procedure with traceable references, and document as-found and as-left data.\nThe procedure covers position sensors, temperature sensors, pressure transmitters, and speed pickups, with the common trap called out: calibrating the sensor while the underlying mechanical fault (a bent bracket, a worn coupling) is still present, which makes the calibration a lie.",
  "h": "Post-repair calibration works because a sensor's output is only meaningful against its mechanical reference: an encoder on a shifted bracket or a pressure transmitter on a restricted impulse line reports confidently wrong numbers. The sequence therefore restores mechanics first, then calibrates electrically. As-found data captures how far the sensor had drifted or shifted, which often explains the original fault; as-left data proves the calibration. Traceable references tie the result to the measurement chain, so the calibration stands up in quality audits. Skipping this step after repair is how machines return to service with new faults wearing the old symptom.",
  "f": ["List every sensor disturbed by the repair - directly or mechanically - and recalibrate each.",
        "Restore the mechanical installation first: position, gap, alignment, brackets.",
        "Record as-found data before adjusting; it often explains the original fault.",
        "Calibrate against traceable references, not against another uncalibrated instrument.",
        "Document as-left data with the reference IDs used.",
        "Never calibrate around an unrepaired mechanical fault - fix mechanics first."],
  "r": ["Calibration procedure and traceable reference standards for each sensor type.",
        "Mechanical restoration: brackets, gaps, and alignment to specification.",
        "As-found/as-left documentation forms.",
        "Stable conditions: temperature soak where the procedure requires it."],
  "s": [0, 6, 2], "c": [2, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("tolerance", {"nominal": 100.0, "tol": 0.5, "unit": "kPa", "readings": [100.2, 99.8, 100.1, 99.9]})},
 {"t": "Firmware Reflash Service Procedure", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Corrupted or outdated firmware causes faults no hardware swap will fix. This record covers the controlled reflash procedure: verifying the fault is firmware-class, obtaining the correct firmware revision from an authorized source, backing up configuration, performing the flash with protected power, and verifying the result including a full functional test.\nThe procedure includes the critical safeguards: never flash the wrong revision, never interrupt power during the write, keep the previous known-good image for rollback, and record the revision change in the equipment history.",
  "h": "Reflashing works because firmware is the machine's behavior: corrupted flash produces erratic faults that mimic hardware failures, and the reflash restores the known-good behavior image. The risk is the write window - an interrupted flash leaves the device without valid firmware, which is why protected, stable power and no interruptions are mandatory. Revision control matters because firmware revisions change behavior and fix lists; flashing an unapproved revision can introduce new faults or void certifications. The backup-and-rollback discipline makes the procedure reversible: if the new revision misbehaves, the known-good image goes back in.",
  "f": ["Confirm the fault is firmware-class before flashing; do not flash as a guess.",
        "Use only the authorized firmware revision for the exact model and hardware revision.",
        "Back up configuration and keep the previous known-good image for rollback.",
        "Guarantee uninterrupted power during the write - an interrupted flash bricks the device.",
        "Record the revision change in the equipment history with date and reason.",
        "Run a full functional test after flashing, not just a power-on check."],
  "r": ["Authorized firmware image with checksum verified against the source.",
        "Manufacturer flashing tool and interface; stable protected power.",
        "Configuration backup storage and rollback image.",
        "Functional test procedure covering the reported fault."],
  "s": [6, 0, 7], "c": [4, 6, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Checksum matches source", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Revision authorized for model", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Config backed up", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Power protected during write", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Functional test passed", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Hydraulic Seal Replacement", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Hydraulic seals fail by wear, hardening, and contamination - and the replacement fails early if the rod or bore that the seal runs on is scored. This record covers seal replacement as a system job: depressurize and lock out, inspect the sealing surfaces, select the exact seal profile and material for the fluid and temperature, install without twisting or nicking, and pressure-test the result.\nThe procedure includes seal identification (profile, size, material code), surface finish requirements, installation tools that prevent lip damage, and the cleanliness discipline that keeps new seals alive.",
  "h": "Seal replacement works because a seal is a system of elastomer, surface, and fluid: the lip needs a smooth hard counter-surface and compatible fluid to survive. Installing a perfect seal on a scored rod guarantees a repeat leak, which is why surface inspection with a fingernail and eyes comes before the new seal goes in. Material compatibility is chemical - the wrong elastomer swells or shrinks in the fluid and fails in weeks. Installation damage is the silent killer: a nicked lip from a sharp edge or a twisted seal from careless fitting leaks from day one. Pressure testing after assembly proves the repair before the machine returns to duty.",
  "f": ["Depressurize fully and lock out; verify zero pressure before opening.",
        "Inspect rod and bore surfaces - a scored surface kills the new seal.",
        "Match seal profile, size, and material code to fluid and temperature.",
        "Install with proper tools; never nick the lip on sharp edges or twist the seal.",
        "Cleanliness is mandatory - one particle under a lip is a leak path.",
        "Pressure-test after assembly and check for weeping at operating temperature."],
  "r": ["Zero-energy verification: pressure gauges read zero, accumulators discharged.",
        "Exact replacement seals: profile, dimensions, material code.",
        "Seal installation tools and surface inspection aids.",
        "Compatible hydraulic fluid for top-up; spill containment."],
  "s": [1, 4, 6], "c": [3, 4, 0],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Depressurize, lock out, verify zero", 15), ("Drain and contain fluid", 20), ("Remove and identify old seals", 20), ("Inspect rod/bore surfaces", 15), ("Install new seals with tools", 30), ("Reassemble, fill, pressure-test", 30)]})},
 {"t": "DC Motor Brush Replacement", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Worn motor brushes cause sparking, speed instability, and eventual commutator damage. This record covers brush replacement before the damage spreads: recognizing brush wear indicators, selecting the correct brush grade, seating new brushes to the commutator, and the run-in procedure.\nThe procedure includes brush grade selection (grade affects commutation and life), spring pressure checks, commutator inspection and cleaning, and the bedding-in run that seats the brush face to the commutator radius.",
  "h": "Brush replacement works because the brush-commutator interface is a sliding electrical contact: the brush must match the commutator's curvature to carry current across its full face. New brushes are flat or pre-radiused approximately; the run-in period wears them to the exact radius, which is why light load running-in matters - full load on unseated brushes causes arcing and pits the commutator. Brush grade is an engineering choice balancing conductivity, friction, and filming: the wrong grade either wears fast or damages the commutator. Spring pressure must be in range: weak springs bounce and arc, stiff springs wear the brush and commutator prematurely.",
  "f": ["Replace brushes at the wear indicator, not after the damage starts.",
        "Use the specified brush grade; grade is an engineering choice, not a commodity.",
        "Inspect and clean the commutator; a damaged commutator destroys new brushes.",
        "Check spring pressure against specification - weak springs arc, stiff springs wear.",
        "Run in new brushes at light load to seat them to the commutator radius.",
        "Replace all brushes as a set; mixed old and new brushes share current unevenly."],
  "r": ["Correct brush grade and size for the motor; full set.",
        "Commutator cleaning tools; mica undercutting check where applicable.",
        "Spring pressure gauge or the specified test method.",
        "Run-in procedure with light, controlled load."],
  "s": [6, 5, 0], "c": [4, 5, 6],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Brush grade matches spec", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Commutator inspected/clean", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Spring pressure in range g", 250.0, 245.0, 30.0), ("Full set replaced", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Run-in completed", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Gasket Replacement and Sealing", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Gaskets seal by controlled compression, and most gasket failures trace to surface preparation or uneven clamping rather than the gasket itself. This record covers the full procedure: selecting the gasket material for the fluid, pressure, and temperature; preparing flange faces; applying sealant only where specified; torquing in sequence and stages; and the retorque after thermal cycling.\nThe procedure includes flange face inspection, the flatness and finish requirements, gasket orientation (which side faces pressure), and the leak test that proves the joint.",
  "h": "Gasket sealing works because the gasket fills the microscopic gaps between flange faces under clamp load; the load must be even or the gasket extrudes on the tight side and leaks on the loose side. Surface preparation dominates: old gasket residue creates high spots that prevent sealing, and scratches across the sealing face become leak paths. Torque sequence and staging even out the clamp load progressively so the gasket compresses uniformly instead of shifting. Retorque after the first thermal cycle compensates for gasket creep and thermal relaxation - the joint that is tight cold can be loose hot, and the retorque is the correction.",
  "f": ["Select gasket material for the actual fluid, pressure, and temperature - all three.",
        "Clean flange faces completely; old residue and scratches are leak paths.",
        "Apply sealant only where the manual specifies; extra sealant squeezes into the system.",
        "Torque in crossing sequence, in stages, to even the clamp load.",
        "Retorque after the first thermal cycle to compensate for creep.",
        "Leak-test at operating pressure and temperature before sign-off."],
  "r": ["Correct gasket: material, dimensions, pressure/temperature rating.",
        "Flange cleaning tools that do not gouge the sealing face.",
        "Calibrated torque wrench; the crossing sequence chart.",
        "Leak test method for the fluid and pressure involved."],
  "s": [4, 6, 0], "c": [3, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("torque", {"fasteners": [("flange bolts DN50", 12, 80.0, 10), ("flange bolts DN80", 16, 140.0, 10), ("cover plate bolts", 8, 25.0, 10)]})},
 {"t": "Shaft Alignment After Repair", "g": "Adjustment", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Any repair that disturbs coupled shafts - bearing replacement, motor swap, gearbox work - requires realignment. This record covers precision shaft alignment: the soft-foot check and correction, rough alignment, then precision measurement with dial indicators or laser, and the shim moves that bring offset and angularity within tolerance.\nThe procedure includes thermal growth allowance, the difference between offset and angular misalignment, tolerance tables by speed, and the verification run that confirms the alignment holds under load.",
  "h": "Shaft alignment works because misalignment forces the coupling to flex each revolution, injecting cyclic loads into both bearings and seals; the forces scale with speed, which is why tolerances tighten as RPM rises. Soft foot - a machine foot not sitting flat - must be corrected first because shimming an alignment on top of soft foot produces an alignment that changes with bolt torque. The measurement (rim-and-face or laser) separates offset from angularity so the correction moves are correct: shimming under feet corrects vertical, moving the machine horizontally corrects lateral. Thermal growth means the cold alignment targets are offset intentionally so the machine grows into alignment at operating temperature.",
  "f": ["Correct soft foot first; aligning over soft foot is wasted work.",
        "Use the tolerance table for the actual operating speed - faster means tighter.",
        "Separate offset from angularity; each needs different correction moves.",
        "Allow for thermal growth: cold targets are offset so the machine grows into alignment.",
        "Verify under load and recheck after the thermal soak.",
        "Record final alignment values; they are the baseline for the next service."],
  "r": ["Dial indicators or laser alignment system, calibrated.",
        "Shim stock in assorted thicknesses; clean shim packs.",
        "Soft-foot measurement before any alignment moves.",
        "Manufacturer alignment tolerances for the speed and coupling type."],
  "s": [5, 6, 2], "c": [4, 5, 3],
  "demo": ("tolerance", {"nominal": 0.0, "tol": 0.05, "unit": "mm offset", "readings": [0.02, -0.03, 0.01, 0.04]})},
 {"t": "Thermal Paste Renewal on Power Electronics", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Dried thermal interface material raises semiconductor junction temperatures until protection trips or devices fail. This record covers renewal: safe discharge and isolation, heatsink removal without device stress, complete old-paste removal, correct new-paste application (thin, even, no voids), and remounting with even pressure.\nThe procedure includes paste selection (thermal conductivity, viscosity, electrical isolation needs), the pea-versus-spread methods by package size, mounting pressure and sequence, and the temperature verification that proves the improvement.",
  "h": "Thermal paste works by filling the microscopic air gaps between the device and heatsink; air is a superb insulator, so even a thin air film dominates the thermal resistance. The paste layer must be thin because the paste itself conducts far worse than the metal - its job is only to displace air. Even mounting pressure matters: uneven pressure tilts the package, creating a wedge of thick paste on one side. Old paste dries by oil separation and becomes a crust with poor contact, which is why complete removal precedes renewal. The proof is temperature: the same load must show lower case or heatsink temperature after the work, measured the same way.",
  "f": ["Discharge and isolate; power devices store charge and bite.",
        "Remove all old paste completely - dried crust is an insulator.",
        "Apply thin and even; the paste displaces air, it is not a gap filler.",
        "Tighten heatsink fasteners evenly in sequence to avoid tilting the package.",
        "Select paste for the need: conductivity, and electrical isolation where required.",
        "Verify by temperature: same load, same measurement point, lower reading after."],
  "r": ["Suitable thermal paste with published conductivity; lint-free wipes and cleaner.",
        "Discharge tools and voltage verification for the power stage.",
        "Torque screwdriver for even heatsink mounting.",
        "Temperature measurement: IR camera or thermocouple at a fixed point."],
  "s": [6, 2, 5], "c": [4, 5, 6],
  "demo": ("case", {"metrics": [("heatsink temp rise", 38.0, 24.0, "C", "down"), ("device case temp", 82.0, 66.0, "C", "down")]})},
 {"t": "Battery Pack Servicing Procedure", "g": "Repair", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Battery packs demand respect: they store large energy in a small volume and fail energetically when mishandled. This record covers servicing where the manufacturer permits it: state-of-charge assessment, safe disconnection sequence, cell or module replacement with matched cells, BMS verification, and the charge-discharge verification cycle.\nThe procedure includes the safety hierarchy (insulated tools, no jewelry, face protection, fire precautions), cell matching requirements, torque on busbars, and the documentation of pack history. Where the manufacturer seals the pack or forbids service, the record directs replacement, not heroics.",
  "h": "Battery servicing works when the pack is treated as an energy system, not a parts bin. Cells in series share current, so a mismatched replacement cell becomes the weak link - overcharged or over-discharged relative to its neighbors, aging fast and risking thermal events. The BMS watches per-cell voltages and temperatures; servicing must leave its sensing intact and verified, because a blind BMS cannot protect the pack. Insulated tools and removed jewelry eliminate the accidental short paths that turn a wrench into an arc flash source. The verification cycle - a controlled charge and discharge with per-cell monitoring - proves the pack is balanced and safe before it returns to duty.",
  "f": ["Assess state of charge first; work on packs at the lowest practical charge.",
        "Insulated tools, no jewelry, face protection - treat every terminal as live.",
        "Replace with matched cells: same chemistry, capacity, and age class.",
        "Verify BMS sensing on every cell after service; a blind BMS protects nothing.",
        "Torque busbars to specification; loose high-current joints overheat.",
        "Run a controlled charge-discharge verification with per-cell monitoring before return to service."],
  "r": ["Manufacturer service authorization for the pack; sealed packs are replaced, not opened.",
        "Insulated hand tools; fire precautions including a suitable extinguisher and isolation area.",
        "Matched replacement cells/modules with traceability.",
        "BMS diagnostic access and a controlled charge/discharge rig."],
  "s": [1, 2, 6], "c": [1, 4, 6],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Pack at safe SoC %", 30.0, 28.0, 10.0), ("Cells matched within 2%", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("BMS sees all cells", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Busbar torque in spec", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Verification cycle passed", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
