 {"t": "Intermittent Fault Hunting Method", "g": "Diagnostics", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Intermittent faults are the hardest class in service: they vanish when the technician arrives. This record teaches the method that catches them: characterize the symptom precisely (when, how often, under what conditions), instrument for unattended capture (data loggers, triggered oscilloscope), apply controlled stresses (heat, cold, vibration, load) to force the fault, and correlate the capture with the conditions.\nThe procedure covers fault journaling with the operator, long-duration monitoring setup, stress sequencing from least to most invasive, and the discipline of changing one variable at a time so the cause is proven, not guessed.",
  "h": "Intermittent hunting works by converting a rare event into a captured event. The fault journal narrows the conditions - time of day, load, temperature, recent maintenance - which suggests the stress to apply. Unattended instrumentation with triggering captures the fault's electrical or thermal signature without a technician standing by for days. Controlled stress then reproduces the fault on demand: heat expands marginal solder joints, cold contracts them, vibration flexes cracked traces, load stresses weak power stages. Changing one variable at a time is the scientific core: when the fault appears only with heat applied, heat is implicated, and the location follows from which heated area triggers it.",
  "f": ["Journal the fault with the operator first: when, how often, under what conditions.",
        "Instrument for unattended capture - data loggers and triggered scopes catch what people miss.",
        "Apply controlled stresses in order: heat, cold, vibration, load - one variable at a time.",
        "Correlate every capture with conditions; the pattern is the diagnosis.",
        "Do not replace parts to hunt intermittents; prove the cause, then repair once.",
        "Verify the fix by repeating the exact stress that reproduced the fault."],
  "r": ["Data logger or triggered oscilloscope for unattended capture.",
        "Controlled stress tools: heat gun, freeze spray, vibration source, load bank.",
        "Operator cooperation for the fault journal.",
        "Patience: intermittents are solved by method, not by speed."],
  "s": [5, 6, 0], "c": [4, 5, 6],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Journal fault with operator", 30), ("Set up unattended capture", 45), ("Review captures, find pattern", 30), ("Apply controlled heat stress", 20), ("Apply vibration stress", 20), ("Localize and repair proven cause", 60), ("Re-stress to verify fix", 30)]})},
 {"t": "Gearbox Overhaul Procedure", "g": "Repair", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "A gearbox overhaul restores the machine's heart: full disassembly, inspection of every gear, bearing, shaft, and housing, replacement of wear parts, and reassembly to specification. This record covers the complete sequence: controlled disassembly with position marking, gear tooth inspection (pitting, spalling, wear patterns), bearing and seal replacement, backlash and contact pattern setup, and the loaded test run.\nThe procedure includes reading wear patterns as diagnostics (what the teeth say about alignment and lubrication), shim-based backlash adjustment, and the oil selection and cleanliness that determine the overhauled box's life.",
  "h": "Gearbox overhaul works because gears and bearings are precision systems: tooth contact patterns reveal alignment, and backlash controls noise, load sharing, and thermal behavior. Marking positions before disassembly preserves the matched relationships the factory set. Wear pattern reading is diagnostic: concentrated contact at one end means misalignment, pitting means fatigue or overload, polishing means good running-in. Backlash set by shimming must suit the operating temperature - gears grow when hot, and a box set tight cold binds hot. The loaded test run with temperature and vibration monitoring proves the overhaul before the machine returns to production.",
  "f": ["Mark every position before disassembly; matched relationships must be restored.",
        "Read gear wear patterns as diagnostics: pitting, spalling, and contact location tell the history.",
        "Replace all bearings and seals during overhaul; they are wear parts.",
        "Set backlash by shimming to specification, allowing for thermal growth.",
        "Fill with the specified oil to the correct level; cleanliness determines life.",
        "Test run under load with temperature and vibration monitoring before handover."],
  "r": ["Full gasket, seal, and bearing kit plus any gears the inspection condemns.",
        "Presses, pullers, and heating equipment for bearing work.",
        "Backlash measurement tools: dial indicators, marking compound.",
        "Specified lubricant; particle-controlled filling."],
  "s": [5, 6, 0], "c": [4, 5, 3],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Backlash within spec mm", 0.15, 0.14, 0.03), ("Contact pattern centered %", 70.0, 72.0, 10.0), ("Oil level correct", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("No abnormal vibration", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Temperature stable", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Spindle Rebuild Procedure", "g": "Repair", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Machine-tool spindles run at speeds where tiny errors become scrap parts. This record covers the rebuild: cleanroom-grade disassembly, bearing and seal replacement with matched precision sets, preload setting, dynamic balancing verification, and the run-in and acceptance test.\nThe procedure includes contamination control (the number one spindle killer), bearing preload methods, drawbar force verification, and the vibration and thermal acceptance criteria that qualify the rebuilt spindle.",
  "h": "Spindle rebuild works because spindle performance is entirely about the bearing system: preload sets stiffness, and stiffness sets accuracy under cutting load. Matched bearing sets are manufactured as pairs with controlled preload; mixing or misordering them destroys the design. Contamination control is extreme because at spindle speeds a single particle dents a race and becomes a vibration source for the bearing's life. Preload must be exact: too little and the spindle chatters, too much and heat kills the bearings. The acceptance test - vibration spectrum, thermal rise, and runout - is the objective proof the rebuild meets specification, measured the same way the manufacturer measures.",
  "f": ["Work clean: contamination is the number one spindle killer - control the environment.",
        "Use matched precision bearing sets in the correct order and orientation.",
        "Set preload exactly to specification; too little chatters, too much overheats.",
        "Verify drawbar force where applicable; weak drawbars cause tool pullout.",
        "Run in per the schedule with temperature monitoring.",
        "Accept on measured criteria: vibration spectrum, thermal rise, runout - not on feel."],
  "r": ["Clean controlled workspace; lint-free handling.",
        "Matched precision bearing set and seals for the exact spindle.",
        "Preload setting tools and drawbar force gauge.",
        "Vibration analyzer and runout measurement for acceptance."],
  "s": [5, 6, 2], "c": [4, 5, 3],
  "demo": ("tolerance", {"nominal": 0.0, "tol": 0.005, "unit": "mm runout", "readings": [0.002, 0.003, 0.001, 0.004]})},
 {"t": "BGA Rework Outline for Service Shops", "g": "Repair", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Ball grid array packages hide their connections underneath, which makes their repair a specialized discipline. This record outlines the service-shop procedure: confirming the BGA is the fault, controlled removal with profiled heat, site preparation and reballing or new-device installation, and X-ray or functional verification.\nThe procedure covers thermal profiles (why they matter), board preheating to prevent warping, moisture sensitivity handling, and the honest limits: BGA rework needs proper equipment, and this record defines the go/no-go for attempting it versus board replacement.",
  "h": "BGA rework works by controlled, profiled heat: the solder balls under the package must all reach liquidus together while the board stays below damage temperature. Board preheating from below reduces the thermal gradient that warps boards and cracks nearby joints. Moisture-sensitive packages must be baked or handled within their floor life, because trapped moisture turns to steam in reflow and delaminates the package. The thermal profile - ramp rate, soak, peak, time above liquidus - is the process: too cool and joints do not form, too hot and the board is damaged. Verification by X-ray shows voids and bridges that no visual check can see.",
  "f": ["Confirm the BGA is the fault first; rework is not a diagnostic step.",
        "Follow a real thermal profile: ramp, soak, peak, time above liquidus.",
        "Preheat the board from below to prevent warping and nearby joint damage.",
        "Respect moisture sensitivity: bake or observe floor life.",
        "Verify with X-ray or thorough functional test - visual checks cannot see under the package.",
        "Know the limit: without proper rework equipment, replace the board."],
  "r": ["BGA rework station with profiled top heat and board preheater.",
        "Thermal profiles for the package and board; thermocouples for verification.",
        "Moisture handling: baking oven and dry storage.",
        "X-ray inspection or a comprehensive functional test."],
  "s": [6, 5, 0], "c": [4, 5, 6],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Fault confirmed at BGA", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Thermal profile followed", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Moisture handling observed", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("X-ray or functional verify", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Root-Cause Failure Analysis Writeup", "g": "Diagnostics", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Fixing the symptom without finding the cause guarantees a repeat failure. This record defines the root-cause failure analysis (RCFA) writeup: evidence preservation, timeline construction, the 5-why or fault-tree analysis, the identified root cause with proof, corrective actions that address the cause (not the symptom), and effectiveness follow-up.\nThe procedure covers what a good writeup contains, how to keep it factual instead of blameful, and the review that turns one failure into prevention across the fleet.",
  "h": "RCFA works by separating the failure mechanism (how it broke) from the root cause (why the mechanism was allowed to happen). The 5-why technique drills past the proximate cause: a bearing failed from no lubrication, because the greasing was missed, because the schedule was unclear, because the procedure was never written - the root cause is the missing procedure, and fixing it prevents the whole class of failures. Evidence preservation matters because the failed part is the primary witness; cleaning or discarding it destroys the data. The effectiveness follow-up closes the loop: corrective actions are verified by the absence of recurrence, not by their completion alone.",
  "f": ["Preserve the failed parts as evidence; do not clean or discard them.",
        "Build a factual timeline before theorizing - sequence first, causes second.",
        "Drill with 5-why or fault tree until reaching a cause whose fix prevents recurrence.",
        "Address the root cause, not the symptom; symptom fixes schedule the next failure.",
        "Write factually, not blamefully - blame shuts down the reporting that RCFA needs.",
        "Follow up: verify the corrective action by the absence of recurrence."],
  "r": ["Failed parts preserved with as-found condition documented.",
        "Timeline data: logs, histories, operator accounts.",
        "A facilitator or reviewer independent of the repair team where possible.",
        "Access to fleet history to check for repeat patterns."],
  "s": [5, 0, 7], "c": [4, 5, 7],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Preserve evidence and document as-found", 30), ("Build failure timeline", 45), ("Apply 5-why/fault-tree analysis", 60), ("Identify root cause with proof", 30), ("Define corrective actions", 30), ("Verify effectiveness on follow-up", 20)]})},
 {"t": "Calibration Restoration After Major Repair", "g": "Adjustment", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Major repairs - gearbox overhauls, spindle rebuilds, structural work - move the machine's geometry, which invalidates every calibration built on that geometry. This record defines the restoration sequence: mechanical acceptance first (alignment, geometry), then instrument recalibration in dependency order, then process verification against a known reference part or artifact.\nThe procedure covers the dependency map (which calibrations rest on which geometry), the order that prevents rework, and the acceptance criteria that qualify the machine for production again.",
  "h": "Calibration restoration works because calibrations form a dependency stack: process accuracy rests on instrument calibration, which rests on machine geometry. Restoring in the wrong order - calibrating instruments on uncorrected geometry - produces calibrations that are wrong the moment geometry is fixed. Mechanical acceptance first proves the machine is geometrically capable; instrument calibration then ties measurements to traceable references; the reference artifact or test part proves the whole stack in the machine's actual working terms. Each layer is verified before the next is built on it, which is what makes the final acceptance trustworthy.",
  "f": ["Map calibration dependencies before starting; restore in dependency order.",
        "Prove mechanical geometry first - alignment, levels, runout - before any instrument calibration.",
        "Recalibrate instruments against traceable references, recording as-found/as-left.",
        "Verify the whole stack with a reference artifact or test part in working terms.",
        "Do not release to production until the acceptance criteria are met and recorded.",
        "Keep the restoration record with the repair record; they are one job."],
  "r": ["Dependency map of calibrations to geometry for the machine.",
        "Traceable reference standards and the reference artifact.",
        "Mechanical acceptance tools: alignment, level, runout.",
        "Production-equivalent test conditions for final verification."],
  "s": [0, 5, 2], "c": [2, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Map calibration dependencies", 20), ("Verify mechanical geometry", 60), ("Recalibrate instruments in order", 90), ("Verify with reference artifact", 45), ("Record and release to production", 20)]})},
 {"t": "EMC Re-Verification After Repair", "g": "Verification", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Repairs change electromagnetic behavior: rerouted cables, replaced shields, different components. This record covers EMC re-verification after repair on equipment where emissions or immunity matter: visual verification of shields, bonds, and routing against the as-designed state, plus functional immunity checks where full lab testing is impractical.\nThe procedure covers what to inspect (shield continuity, 360-degree terminations, filter placement, cable segregation), the simple field checks that catch most regressions, and when to require formal re-testing.",
  "h": "EMC re-verification works because electromagnetic compatibility is a property of the whole assembly: shields, filters, grounding, and routing form a system, and a repair that disturbs any element can undo the design. A shield with a pigtail ground instead of a 360-degree termination is a different antenna; a power cable rerouted alongside a signal cable couples noise the design kept apart. Visual verification against the as-designed state catches most regressions because EMC design is largely geometry. Field functional checks - operating the equipment through its modes while monitoring for anomalies - catch immunity regressions where lab testing is out of reach. Formal re-testing is required when the repair changed the EMC-critical design or when compliance is certified.",
  "f": ["Restore shields, bonds, filters, and routing exactly to the as-designed state.",
        "Verify shield continuity and proper terminations - pigtails are not terminations.",
        "Keep power and signal cable segregation as designed.",
        "Run functional immunity checks through all operating modes after repair.",
        "Require formal re-testing when the repair changed EMC-critical design or certification applies.",
        "Photograph the restored EMC details; they are part of the repair record."],
  "r": ["As-designed EMC documentation: shield, bond, filter, and routing details.",
        "Continuity and bonding measurement tools.",
        "Functional test procedure covering all operating modes.",
        "Criteria for when formal lab re-testing is mandatory."],
  "s": [6, 0, 7], "c": [4, 6, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Shields restored as designed", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Bond resistance < 0.1 ohm", 0.05, 0.04, 0.05), ("Cable segregation kept", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Functional modes all pass", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Safety Interlock Restoration and Test", "g": "Verification", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Guards get removed for access and interlocks get bypassed for convenience - both must be fully restored and proven before the machine returns to service. This record covers the restoration: verifying every guard is present and secure, every interlock switch actuates correctly, the control system responds by stopping hazardous motion, and the whole chain is tested in every mode.\nThe procedure includes the defeat check (the interlock must not be defeatable by simple means), response-time verification, and the documentation that proves the safety system works. A machine with an untested interlock is not ready for service.",
  "h": "Interlock restoration works because safety is a chain: guard, switch, wiring, control logic, and actuator must all function, and the chain is only as strong as its weakest link. Testing each link separately then as a system catches the wiring fault that a switch-only test misses. The defeat check matters because interlocks defeated with tape or jumpers are a leading cause of serious injuries; the design must resist casual defeat and the test must confirm it. Response-time verification ensures the machine stops before a person can reach the hazard - distance and stopping time are the physics of guarding. Documented testing creates the proof that the safety system was working when the machine was handed over.",
  "f": ["Restore every guard and interlock removed or bypassed during the repair - no exceptions.",
        "Test each link: switch actuation, wiring, control response, actuator stop.",
        "Verify the interlock cannot be defeated by simple means.",
        "Check stopping performance: the machine must stop before the hazard is reachable.",
        "Test in every operating mode, including setup and maintenance modes.",
        "Document the tests; an untested interlock is an unproven safety system."],
  "r": ["Interlock test procedure for the machine's safety functions.",
        "Means to measure stopping time/distance where applicable.",
        "No bypass devices present; defeated interlocks removed.",
        "Sign-off by a competent person before return to service."],
  "s": [1, 6, 7], "c": [0, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("All guards present and secure", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Each interlock actuates correctly", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Control stops hazardous motion", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Not defeatable by simple means", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Tested in all modes", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Pressure System Repair Documentation", "g": "Repair", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Pressure equipment repairs carry legal and safety weight beyond the wrench work: the repair must be documented to the applicable code, performed by qualified persons, and verified by the prescribed tests. This record covers the documentation package: the repair plan, materials with traceability, welder or technician qualifications, examination records, pressure test records, and the updated equipment file.\nThe procedure follows the logic of ASME PCC-2 and jurisdictional rules: what repairs need engineering review, what examinations apply, and what the test pressure and hold time must be.",
  "h": "Pressure repair documentation works because pressure vessels store energy proportional to pressure times volume - a failure is violent, so the rules are strict and written. Traceability ties every material and weld back to its source and qualification, which is what lets an inspector trust a repair years later. The pressure test at the prescribed multiple of working pressure proves the repair's integrity with margin; the hold time lets small leaks show. Engineering review for repairs beyond the routine recognizes that pressure boundaries are designed systems - changing them without analysis risks the design margin. The updated equipment file keeps the vessel's history complete for its entire service life.",
  "f": ["Follow the applicable code (ASME PCC-2) and jurisdictional requirements - they are law, not guidance.",
        "Use qualified persons for pressure-boundary work; document the qualifications.",
        "Maintain material traceability: every part back to its source.",
        "Pressure-test at the prescribed pressure and hold time; witness and record it.",
        "Get engineering review for any repair beyond the routine scope.",
        "Update the equipment file; the vessel's history must be complete for life."],
  "r": ["Applicable code and jurisdictional requirements identified before work starts.",
        "Qualified welders/technicians with current credentials.",
        "Calibrated test gauges and examination equipment.",
        "The equipment file, updated with the full repair package."],
  "s": [4, 1, 0], "c": [3, 0, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Repair plan approved", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Personnel qualified", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Materials traceable", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Test pressure held min", 30.0, 32.0, 5.0), ("Equipment file updated", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Field Repair Under Constraints", "g": "Repair", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Sometimes the repair must happen where the machine stands, with limited tools, no parts crib, and production waiting. This record covers the discipline of constrained field repair: rapid but complete diagnosis, the minimum safe repair that restores function, temporary-versus-permanent classification, and the follow-up work order that finishes the job properly.\nThe procedure includes the field kit that makes constrained repair possible, the safety lines that are never crossed for speed, and the documentation that ensures a temporary repair is never forgotten into permanence.",
  "h": "Constrained repair works by separating the immediate need (restore function safely) from the complete need (restore to specification). The minimum safe repair is an engineering judgment: it must not introduce new hazards or mask a fault that will fail worse later. Classifying the repair as temporary with a dated follow-up work order is the control that prevents temporary fixes from becoming permanent liabilities - the classification, not the technician's memory, drives the follow-up. The safety lines are absolute because field pressure is exactly when shortcuts injure people: lockout, pressure, electrical, and structural safeties are never negotiable. A stocked field kit converts improvisation into procedure.",
  "f": ["Diagnose completely even under pressure; a rushed wrong repair costs more than a careful one.",
        "Define the minimum safe repair - safe is non-negotiable, minimum is the judgment.",
        "Classify temporary vs permanent in writing, with a dated follow-up work order.",
        "Never cross safety lines for speed: lockout, pressure, electrical, structural.",
        "A temporary repair without a follow-up date is a permanent liability - document it.",
        "Stock the field kit so constrained repair is procedure, not improvisation."],
  "r": ["Field kit: diagnostics, common parts, fasteners, sealants, documentation.",
        "Authority to classify temporary repairs and raise follow-up work orders.",
        "Communication to operations: what was done, limits, and follow-up date.",
        "The same safety equipment as shop repair - constraints never reduce safety."],
  "s": [1, 6, 0], "c": [0, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Rapid complete diagnosis", 20), ("Define minimum safe repair", 10), ("Execute repair with field kit", 60), ("Classify temp/permanent in writing", 10), ("Raise dated follow-up work order", 10), ("Brief operations on limits", 10)]})},
 {"t": "Repair-Versus-Replace Decision Record", "g": "Diagnostics", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "Every major failure poses the economic question: repair or replace? This record defines the decision method: total cost of repair (parts, labor, downtime, risk of recurrence) versus total cost of replacement (price, installation, commissioning, training), adjusted for remaining useful life, reliability history, and parts availability.\nThe procedure includes the cost worksheet, the risk factors that numbers miss (safety, obsolescence, efficiency gains), and the decision record format that makes the reasoning auditable.",
  "h": "The decision method works because it compares total costs, not price tags: a cheap repair that fails again costs more than a replacement, and a new machine's commissioning cost can dwarf its price. Remaining useful life is the key normalizer - spending half the replacement cost to gain one year is different from gaining ten. Reliability history adjusts the repair cost by the probability of recurrence; a machine with chronic failures carries a risk premium on every repair. The documented record matters because these decisions are revisited: when the repaired machine fails again, the record shows whether the decision was sound or the analysis was wrong, which improves the next decision.",
  "f": ["Compare total costs: repair (parts, labor, downtime, recurrence risk) vs replacement (price, install, commission).",
        "Normalize by remaining useful life - cost per reliable year is the metric.",
        "Add the risk premium from reliability history; chronic failures tax every repair.",
        "Include the non-numbers: safety, obsolescence, efficiency, parts availability.",
        "Document the decision and reasoning; auditability improves future decisions.",
        "Revisit when facts change: a new failure mode or a parts discontinuation resets the math."],
  "r": ["Cost data: parts, labor rates, downtime cost, replacement pricing.",
        "Reliability history and parts availability assessment.",
        "Remaining-life estimate from condition or age.",
        "Decision record template with the full reasoning."],
  "s": [0, 5, 7], "c": [4, 5, 7],
  "demo": ("bom", {"items": [("repair parts", 1, 2400.0), ("repair labor 16h", 1, 1440.0), ("downtime 8h", 1, 4000.0)], "labor_h": 0, "rate": 0, "expect_total": 7840.0})},
 {"t": "Final Acceptance Test Protocol", "g": "Verification", "tier": "advanced",
  "d": "The acceptance test is the repair's final exam: a defined protocol that proves the equipment meets its specification before handover. This record defines the protocol structure: test conditions, measured parameters with pass criteria, the sequence from no-load to full-load, safety checks, and the sign-off.\nThe procedure covers writing pass criteria from the specification (not from hope), the load progression that reveals faults safely, recording actual values (not just pass/fail), and the handover package that accompanies the tested machine.",
  "h": "Acceptance testing works because it replaces opinion with measurement: the machine either meets its specified parameters or it does not. Load progression from no-load to full-load is the safety logic - faults reveal themselves at increasing severity, and catching them at low load prevents damage. Recording actual values instead of checkmarks creates a baseline for the next service and proves the test was real. Pass criteria taken from the specification (not invented for the test) keep the standard honest. The sign-off transfers responsibility formally: the machine is declared fit for service by a named person on a recorded date, which is the accountability the whole service process builds toward.",
  "f": ["Write pass criteria from the equipment specification before testing.",
        "Progress from no-load to full-load; let faults reveal at safe severity.",
        "Record actual measured values, not just pass/fail - they are the next baseline.",
        "Include safety system tests in the protocol, every time.",
        "Test under the conditions of the original fault, not just nominal conditions.",
        "Sign off formally: named person, date, measured results, fit-for-service declaration."],
  "r": ["Equipment specification with the parameters and tolerances to prove.",
        "Calibrated test instruments for every measured parameter.",
        "Load means: from no-load through rated load safely.",
        "Acceptance protocol form with sign-off fields."],
  "s": [0, 6, 2], "c": [4, 6, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("No-load run normal", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Full-load parameters in spec", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Safety systems tested", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Original fault condition tested", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Values recorded", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Signed fit for service", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
